  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 159 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nHeight rises on the exiting half of the shortwave will suppress\nrain chances Sunday and the main moisture axis will be pulled\ntowards the lee side troughing. Southeast and eastern Iowa may \nsee some very light rain early. Highs will start to recover into\nthe upper 60s and near 70, but a prolonged period of rain and\nsome storms will arrive to start next week. The southern stream\nwave will eject into the plains, enhancing the low and midlevel\njet in the warm sector. At this range, deterministic runs bring\nMUCAPE values around the 300 J/kg range into southern Iowa. As\nmentioned yesterday, the potential for surface-based severe\nweather is heavily challenged by morning rain and lingering\ncloud cover. Moderate wrap-around rain stays in the state for\nTuesday and partially into Wednesday. NBM QMD guidance shows \nthat much of the state has around a 50% chance of exceeding 2\" \nof rain, which matches the early convective nature as well as \nthe very high PWATs (1.5\") by November standards. The \nprogressive nature of convection and the long-duration of rain \nwill reduce potential hydrologic impacts. Highs will cool into the\n50s following the system's departure and the upstream pattern\nwill remain active and amplified.",
